edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1045 "Inhibited Decay Models With First-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078671 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "99731adb-b075-5d4b-865c-6112280a68cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078879 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078879 #edb/ref 1071 "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{∘}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58a15628-6a07-582f-a2d4-e1c7a7c6e77c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078879 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079538 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079538 #edb/ref 1061 584874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079538 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b8000c0-e0de-53fb-a0bd-c942ac0f5e81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079538 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079539 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079539 #edb/ref 1061 584875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079539 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21c46d35-42c5-51bc-8679-d81c11066483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079539 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079540 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079540 #edb/ref 1061 584880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079540 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2253199d-d2ca-53d8-a2f6-aa2bd0cd82fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079540 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079541 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079541 #edb/ref 1061 584881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079541 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f3084d6-abb3-5bf6-aedb-e65df0e1523b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079541 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079542 #edb/ref 1061 584884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079542 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ae0595c0-ae02-56d3-a449-bb3345ccbe25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079542 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079555 #edb/ref 1061 608082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7feac704-66a6-58a5-be02-6d1d595f029b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079619 #edb/ref 1064 "From the previous slide, the temperature of the thermometer is given by\n\n$$\nT = 20 + Ke^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ and $k$ are constants to be determined.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the water was $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0) = 100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =20+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =20+K \\\\ K & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following expression for $T(t){:}$\n\n$$\nT(t) = 20 + 80e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $k$ using the fact that $T(7)=60{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =20+80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ 40 & =80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-7k \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =-7k \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature, in $^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ of the thermometer after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT = 20 + 80e^{-t\\ln(2)/7}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079619 #edb/ref 1065 14906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079619 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7811364e-9b00-5478-8f01-6f3ddfee6f58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079619 #edb/ref 1067 "Newton's Law of Cooling (Continued)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079671 #edb/ref 1064 "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited decay models, let’s look at a classic example: **Newton’s law of cooling**.\n\nThis law describes how the temperature of an object changes when it is placed in an environment with a different temperature. Formally, it states that\n\n*The rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings.*\n\nThis leads to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nLet’s put this into action. Suppose a thermometer is placed in a pot of boiling water and reads $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ The pot is then placed in a $20^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ room, and the thermometer's reading begins to decrease.\n\nGiven that after $7$ minutes its reading is $60^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ let's model the thermometer's temperature after $t$ minutes.\n\nIn this case, the temperature of the surroundings is $T_S = 20^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, by Newton's law of cooling, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-20),\n$$\n\nwhere $T(t)$ is the temperature reading of the thermometer and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-20| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-20 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-20 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-20 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe'll determine the value of $K$ and $k$ in the next slide." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079671 #edb/ref 1065 4075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079671 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f58653bc-cafb-532a-b92d-8183054c181b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079671 #edb/ref 1067 "Newton's Law of Cooling" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079684 #edb/ref 1064 "In many real situations, a quantity decreases quickly at first, but then the decrease slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, consider a cup of hot coffee placed in a room. At first, the coffee cools rapidly because it is much hotter than the surrounding air. As time passes and the coffee temperature gets closer to room temperature, the cooling slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature rather than continuing to decrease.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited decay model**.\n\nAn *inhibited decay equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ decreases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as a lower bound. The solution decreases over time but never drops below $a,$ instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/1b/1b68f547db4090382b3fee626d029c619448b1c9adafe8b50312d162ff85e623.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited decay models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited decay, we factorise out the *negative* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 20-5P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited decay equation for $P > 4$ and $t > 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=5$ and $a=4$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = -5(P-4).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079684 #edb/ref 1065 5513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079684 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "da7cd802-d46b-546f-bdc3-8a783a8bb999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079684 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241219 #edb/ref 1080 "{198}^{∘}\\text{F}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241219 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241219 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04eda6ac-1a0c-52d3-accd-9e4287c8ed9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241380 #edb/ref 1080 "{223}^{∘}\\text{F}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241380 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241380 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1769c61b-0297-5fb6-9734-e3f5580daf02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242096 #edb/ref 1080 "{194}^{∘}\\text{F}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242096 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242096 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "65c47c3d-f35e-5489-ade5-8d1e2e7968d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242193 #edb/ref 1080 "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242193 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242193 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "728037c7-4e08-5de7-8ff2-e11877f70cbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243147 #edb/ref 1080 "{189}^{∘}\\text{F}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243147 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243147 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dcfe89bb-415b-5c7b-84d6-7bacf9027ad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243547 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243547 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243547 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186242193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243547 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "393a690d-1e4c-5074-9abc-2b65ddbc8354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244026 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244026 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244026 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244026 #edb/ref 1071 "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh-baked cake can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the cake is ${340}^{∘}F,$ calculate the temperature of the cake after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f919d480-6159-52e5-bf01-76d804f5fa17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244026 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that the temperature of the fresh-baked cake is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-70| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-70>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-70 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-70 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-70 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =70+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the cake was ${340}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=340,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}340 & =70+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 340 & =70+K \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=70+270{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the cake after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=70+270{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 8}≈{201}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321542 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-64(T-100)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "342581f4-cabc-5059-a251-2692ca6f27ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322040 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-100(T-64)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322040 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322040 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71be98e8-1e44-50bb-9bb9-b32dd2edc859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322796 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-100)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322796 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322796 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d2e6a8e3-742a-59bc-850f-65fe5a3e9e9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322949 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-164)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322949 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322949 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e62e99ed-fb8f-592b-af00-7b9afa2790e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323010 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323010 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323010 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ec9ef3f2-b765-5498-b81e-dd99e9dc25d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186323010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323328 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323328 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323328 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186323010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323328 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "53b01d91-18e1-599b-8abf-64d7291ac4f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397884 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397884 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397884 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397884 #edb/ref 1071 "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${60}^{∘}C.$ After $10$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${52}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${40}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${28}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397884 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fee906ee-def7-5194-a87e-a1f77108aa56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397884 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={28}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${60}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=60,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 60 & =28+K \\\\ K & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+32{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${52}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(10)=52,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =28+32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ 24 & =32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-10k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${40}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =40 \\\\ 28+32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =40 \\\\ 32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =12 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${40}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈34\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397885 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397885 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 